  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Investigation\u202fsummary \u2013 Disruption at\u202fXiamen Tungsten (first reported\u202f22\u202fSept\u202f2010)**  \n\n| Tier | Company (sector) | Why it appears in the supply\u2011graph | Evidence found | What could not be confirmed |\n|------|-------------------|------------------------------------|----------------|-----------------------------|\n| **Tier\u202f1** (direct downstream customers) | Zhejiang Founder Motor (Automotive \u2013 EV drive/traction motors) | Listed as a direct downstream customer of Xiamen Tungsten by the supply\u2011graph algorithm. | *No filing or corpus mention* that ties this firm to the 2010 disruption. | No public filing, news item or SEC\u2010document reference was located for this company in the limited tool calls available. |\n| | Sumitomo Electric Industries (Automotive \u2013 wiring harnesses) | Direct downstream customer. | *None* found. | No corroborating evidence of impact. |\n| | TDK (Technology\u2011hardware \u2013 passive components) | Direct downstream customer; also a \u201cenriched\u201d company in the graph. | **Search\u2011corpus result** shows that TDK owns **TDK\u202fXiamen Co., Ltd.**, a subsidiary that manufactures electronic components in Xiamen, China. The excerpt (see tool output) lists this subsidiary among TDK\u2019s many overseas plants and confirms TDK\u2019s operational presence at the same location as Xiamen Tungsten. This is the only concrete piece of documentation linking a downstream firm to the Xiamen site. | No filing from TDK that specifically mentions tungsten supply or any disruption in 2010\u201109\u201122 timeframe. |\n| | BYD (Automotive \u2013 battery\u2011electric vehicles) | Direct downstream customer. | **Get\u2011supplier\u2011info** call returned \u201cNo filings on record for \u2018BYD\u2019\u201d for the relevant date range. | No SEC filing, press release or other document confirming that BYD was affected by the Xiamen Tungsten event. |\n| | CALB (Automotive \u2013 Li\u2011ion battery cells) | Direct downstream customer. | *None* found. | No public evidence of impact. |\n| | Panasonic (Technology\u2011hardware \u2013 automotive lithium\u2011ion cells) | Direct downstream customer. | *None* found. | No corroborating documentation. |\n| | \u2026 *(other Tier\u202f1 firms listed in the traversal output such as Contemporary Amperex Technology, Gotion High\u2011Tech, Sunwoda Electronic, Samsung SDI, Murata Manufacturing, Hunan Corun New Energy, etc.)* | All appear as direct downstream customers according to the graph. | *None* of these companies produced any filing or corpus hit within the four tool calls we were allowed. | Their exposure remains **structural only** \u2013 no specific evidence confirming that they felt a material impact from the Xiamen Tungsten disruption. |\n| **Tier\u202f2** (customers of Tier\u202f1 firms, or alternative suppliers) | Nissan Motor, Toyota Motor, Volkswagen, Daimler Truck Holding, etc. | Appear two hops away in the graph; therefore at risk if their Tier\u20111 suppliers were hit. | *None* found for any Tier\u20112 firm. | No direct evidence that the disruption propagated beyond Tier\u202f1. |\n| \u2026 *(the full Tier\u202f2 list is extensive \u2013 see the traversal output for 45+ companies ranging from automotive OEMs to aluminium\u2011alloy producers, construction firms, oil & gas majors, etc.)* | | | | |\n\n### What the evidence tells us\n\n1. **Structural exposure** \u2013 The supply\u2011graph algorithm identifies **more than 100 downstream entities** (105 \u201cenriched\u201d companies, 302 reachable) within two tiers of Xiamen Tungsten. All of these firms could be affected in principle because they source tungsten or tungsten\u2011based components from the disrupted plant.\n\n2. **Concrete documentation** \u2013 The only concrete piece of public information retrieved with the limited tool budget is the **TDK\u202fXiamen Co., Ltd.** reference (search\u2011corpus result). This confirms that TDK had a manufacturing presence in Xiamen, making it plausible that TDK\u2019s component production could have been impacted by any operational issue at Xiamen Tungsten.\n\n3. **No filing\u2011level confirmation** \u2013 Attempts to pull SEC filings for two high\u2011profile downstream firms (BYD and Tesla) returned \u201cno filings on record\u201d for the relevant date range, meaning we cannot verify from a company\u2019s own reporting whether they disclosed any supply\u2011chain disruption in September\u202f2010.\n\n4. **No additional corpus hits** \u2013 The free\u2011text search across all filings did not surface any other company explicitly mentioning Xiamen Tungsten, a shortage of tungsten, or a related supply interruption around the 22\u202fSept\u202f2010 date.\n\n### Gaps & incomplete parts of the investigation\n\n- **Major Tier\u202f1 automotive and battery makers (e.g., BYD, CALB, Panasonic, Samsung SDI, Murata, etc.)** are listed as downstream customers but we have **no public filing or news reference** confirming that they experienced any material impact. Their risk status remains *theoretical* based on the graph alone.\n\n- **Tier\u202f2 and beyond** (OEMs such as Nissan, Volkswagen, Daimler, etc.) are even further removed; without additional filings or news items we cannot assess whether any knock\u2011on effects occurred.\n\n- The **search\u2011corpus query was limited to \u201cXiamen Tungsten\u201d**, which returned only generic material about TDK\u2019s Xiamen subsidiary and unrelated aluminium\u2011company disclosures. It did not capture any press releases, incident reports, or supplier notices that might have been filed under a different phrasing (e.g., \u201ctungsten shortage\u201d, \u201cXiamen plant outage\u201d). Because we exhausted the four\u2011call budget, those potential sources remain unchecked.\n\n### Bottom line\n\n- **Structural at\u2011risk companies**: All Tier\u202f1 and Tier\u202f2 firms listed in the traversal output are *potentially* exposed to the Xiamen Tungsten disruption. The list includes dozens of major automotive OEMs (BYD, Tesla, Nissan, Volkswagen, etc.), battery manufacturers (CALB, Panasonic, Samsung SDI, Sunwoda), component makers (TDK, Murata, Sumitomo Electric), and many others.\n\n- **Confirmed evidence**: Only TDK\u2019s Xiamen subsidiary is directly documented in the public filings we accessed, indicating a tangible link to the Xiamen location. No other company provided filing\u2011level or corpus evidence of being affected at that time.\n\n- **Unconfirmed impact**: For the vast majority of identified downstream firms (including high\u2011profile EV makers and component suppliers) we could not locate any SEC filing, press release, or other public document confirming a disruption effect. Consequently, while they are structurally vulnerable, the actual material impact on their operations remains **unverified** within the limits of this investigation.\n\n> **Result:** The investigation identifies a large network of companies that could be at risk from the Xiamen Tungsten event, but with only one concrete public reference (TDK\u202fXiamen) and no filing\u2011level confirmations for the other significant downstream firms. Further research\u2014beyond the four\u2011call limit used here\u2014would be required to determine whether any of these companies reported supply shortages, production delays, or financial impacts linked to the September\u202f2010 disruption.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (103) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - zhejiang founder motor: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo electric industries: no evidence gathered\n  - byd: no evidence gathered\n  - calb: no evidence gathered\n  - contemporary amperex technology: no evidence gathered\n  - gotion high-tech: no evidence gathered\n  - sunwoda electronic: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung sdi: no evidence gathered\n  - murata manufacturing: no evidence 